Docker Engine and container tools

Install:
  Install missing Docker packages and NVIDIA container support when an NVIDIA GPU is present.
  On each installation, add the invoking non-root user to the Docker group; sudo uses the original user, and direct root installations skip user access.
  Configure an optional local HTTP proxy for the Docker daemon, including when Docker is already installed.
  Enable automatic build cache GC with a maximum used space of at most 100GB, preserving stricter existing limits, including when Docker is already installed.
  On a new engine installation, enable and restart Docker.

Upgrade:
  Update installed Docker and NVIDIA container packages, retaining service and daemon proxy settings.
  When Docker Engine is installed, install missing configuration tools and apply the 100GB build cache GC configuration.

Status:
  Report installed package versions, Docker service activity, startup state, daemon proxy file presence and build cache GC file settings.

Requirements:
  Ubuntu, APT, a running systemd instance and root or sudo access.

Configuration:
  Daemon proxy configuration: /etc/systemd/system/docker.service.d/wsley-proxy.conf
  Daemon configuration: /etc/docker/daemon.json
  Build cache GC applies to the default docker driver; separate BuildKit builders retain their own configuration.
  Without custom policies, set a maximum used space of at most 100GB and preserve existing reserved-space and free-space settings; reduce reserved space only when it exceeds the resulting maximum.
  Missing reserved-space and free-space settings use Docker-style 10% and 20% targets from the build cache filesystem under data-root, rounded to whole decimal GB; reserved space is capped at the maximum used space.
  Existing custom policies retain their order and conditions; append an unfiltered all-cache rule with reservedSpace=100GB and maxUsedSpace=100GB when that rule is absent. Stricter existing policies still apply.
  The total limit takes precedence over custom retention rules above 100GB; rules may still clean earlier according to their own conditions.
  GC runs periodically and also follows Docker's cache-age rules; 100GB is a cleanup threshold, not a hard disk quota.
  The optional daemon proxy uses HTTP at 127.0.0.1; existing proxy configuration is preserved.
  Leave the port empty to use 7897, enter 0 to skip or enter a port between 1 and 65535; --yes does not answer this prompt.
  Docker daemon proxy settings apply to image pulls; proxy settings inside containers are configured separately.
  NO_PROXY defaults include localhost, the Docker host and private networks.
  Existing runtime configuration is preserved; a missing file receives NVIDIA runtime settings when NVIDIA container support is newly installed.
  Changed daemon configuration is validated before replacement and existing content is backed up; unchanged configuration is not rewritten or backed up.
  Status reads file settings and does not establish whether the running daemon has loaded them; use docker buildx inspect default after restarting to inspect active GC policies.
  Restart an existing Docker engine to apply daemon proxy, GPU runtime or build cache GC changes; existing engines are not restarted automatically by the configuration step.
  Containers connect directly to the upstream proxy. On native Ubuntu, host.docker.internal:host-gateway maps the Docker host; the proxy must listen on a container-reachable address.
  In WSL mirrored mode, direct access to a Windows proxy requires hostAddressLoopback=true in .wslconfig and a container mapping to the current Windows IPv4. Docker host-gateway still maps the Linux bridge.
  The upstream proxy application and its listener are administrator-managed.
  Docker group membership grants root-equivalent access. Log out and back in after membership changes.
